<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_send — paper-plane / send-arrow icon
    ===
    Referenced by:
      - activity_chat.xml (btn_send, via app:icon)
      - future: msg_forward_to dialog (send action)
      - future: attach-preview dialog (send action)

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       Single fill color (#FFFFFFFF), tinted at the consumer
       level (MaterialButton's app:iconTint="?attr/colorOnPrimary"
       in activity_chat.xml, resolving to white in light mode
       and the deep primary-on-dark indigo in dark mode). Do
       NOT set a brand color here — the fill is a template.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT.
       Matches every other chrome icon in the app.
       MaterialButton's app:iconSize="20dp" scales this
       losslessly to 20dp — the send button's declared icon
       size — while the vector's stroke weight and proportions
       remain crisp.

    3. STANDARD MATERIAL GLYPH.
       This is the canonical Material Design "send" icon:
       a filled arrowhead pointing up-and-to-the-right, with
       a small triangular notch at the lower-left (the
       "fold" that suggests a paper plane). The notched
       construction is what distinguishes it from a plain
       arrow — a simple arrow would read as "forward" or
       "navigate," not "send."

       Do NOT redesign this as a full paper-plane outline
       (Telegram style), a filled arrow with a tail (iOS
       arrow style), or an envelope icon. The Material send
       glyph is what users of every Material app recognize,
       and it is what fits the filled MaterialButton
       context best.

    4. SINGLE FILLED PATH.
       The send glyph is a solid shape, not an outlined one.
       At 20dp inside a filled button, an outlined send icon
       would appear visually thin and "floating." The Material
       standard for filled buttons is a solid icon. The
       fill-type is default (nonZero), not evenOdd — there
       are no interior holes in this shape.

    5. NO androidx.AutoMirrored.
       The send glyph points up-and-to-the-right. In RTL
       locales, the standard Material convention is NOT to
       mirror it — the "send = up-right" direction is
       universally understood as "dispatch," and mirroring
       to "up-left" would confuse users who have been trained
       by every other app that up-right means send. Do not
       add auto-mirroring (unlike the back arrow, which does
       need it).

    Rendering result at 20dp (send-button icon size):
      A crisp up-right-pointing arrow with a notched tail,
      immediately readable as "send this message."
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        Canonical Material Design "send" path.

        Geometry:
          - Arrow tip: (2, 21) — the lower-left origin
          - Upper-right corner: (23, 12) — the arrowhead's
            upper-right edge
          - Lower-right corner: (2, 3) — the arrowhead's
            lower-right edge, mirrored vertically
          - Notch point: (6, 12) — the "fold" that creates
            the paper-plane look

        The path traces the outline of the shape:
          M2,21       start at lower-left
          L23,12      diagonal up-right to the tip's
                      upper-right corner
          L2,3        diagonal down-left to the tip's
                      lower-right corner
          L6,12       up-right to the notch
          Z           close back to start

        Rendered: a filled arrowhead with a notched tail
        — the standard Material "send" glyph. The 6,12 notch
        is the "fold" and produces the paper-plane
        silhouette without requiring a separate stroke.
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M2,21 L23,12 L2,3 L2,10 L17,12 L2,14 Z" />

</vector>
